\DOC LOCAL_SUB_SM2_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SUB_SM2_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic-execution goal across an inlined modular-subtraction
subroutine, replacing the current machine-state assumptions with ones about a
later state and adding a conditional specification for the computed difference.

\DESCRIBE
LOCAL_SUB_SM2_TAC takes an {int}, an offset/index used in locating the
subroutine call site, and a {string list} naming, in order, the result operand
and the two operands of the subtraction; these names fix the terms appearing in
the added specification assumption. The goal's conclusion is an {eventually}
statement about a state variable, and it is rewritten with a fresh, later state
variable. All assumptions describing the old state — program counter value,
{bytes_loaded}, register and memory contents, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE}
relation — are discharged and replaced by corresponding assumptions about the
new state, the {MAYCHANGE} component possibly extended by further memory
components. One new assumption is also introduced: an implication whose
antecedent bounds the two input values and whose conclusion equates the named
result to the modular difference of the inputs. The specification assumption
added previously is retained, though reordered among the assumptions.
{
   A ?- t
   ========================================  LOCAL_SUB_SM2_TAC n ["res"; "op1"; "op2"]
   A' ?- t'
}
\noindent Here {t'} is {t} with a fresh later state variable, and {A'} is {A}
with the old-state facts swapped for new-state ones plus the new difference
specification.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, the tactic performs exactly this single-subgoal
transformation on symbolic-execution goals of this shape.

\ENDDOC
